from review import card,save,DATE

i='TCS-3361';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 1.1, Theorems 2 and 4; Section 5, final communication question.'
refs += [dict(id='full',title='Pure Differentially Private Summation from Anonymous Messages — full version',authors=refs[0]['authors'],year=2020,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.01919',locator='Noninteractive model; communication lower bound and Section 6, interaction question.'),dict(id='aggregation2024',title='Pure-DP Aggregation in the Shuffle Model: Error-Optimal and Communication-Efficient',authors='Badih Ghazi; Ravi Kumar; Pasin Manurangsi',year=2024,url='https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ITC.2024.4',locator='Theorems 2 and 6; Section 6, bounded versus expected communication.')]
issue='The source does not specify an interactive shuffle model or the quantitative meaning of a nontrivial lower bound. Fix who may send adaptive queries, whether a user can participate repeatedly, the visible transcript and metadata, public randomness, the number of rounds, and which messages count toward each user’s worst-case bit cost. Then choose the required lower-bound function and error regime. Do not silently replace this request by the stronger claim that the noninteractive square-root-logarithmic bound survives all interaction.'
save(i,dict(title='Communication lower bounds for interactive pure-private shuffled summation',formal='What lower bound on worst-case per-user communication is necessary for interactive pure ε-differentially private binary summation in the shuffle model? There are n users with bits x₁,…,xₙ, and an analyst estimates s(x)=Σᵢxᵢ. The antecedent is the source’s noninteractive lower bound Ω_{ε,χ}(√log n) bits per user for expected absolute error at most n^{1/2−χ}, with fixed ε>0 and χ>0; in particular it rules out constant communication at error bounded independently of n. The source asks whether any nontrivial communication lower bound persists with interaction. Its interaction rules and the requested strength of the bound remain unspecified, so this is not yet a single complete asymptotic target.',definitions='A noninteractive shuffle protocol consists of a local randomized encoder, independently applied to each user’s bit, a trusted shuffler that uniformly permutes the resulting messages and removes sender identities, and an analyst that estimates the sum from the shuffled messages. Multiple messages from one user are allowed. For a complete analyst-visible transcript T, pure ε differential privacy requires Pr[T(x)∈E]≤exp(ε)Pr[T(x′)∈E] for every measurable event E and all x,x′∈{0,1}ⁿ differing in one coordinate. Probabilities include all protocol randomness; there is no additive δ allowance. Privacy must hold for the exposed transcript, not merely the final estimate. Expected absolute error at most a(n) means sup_x E[|ŝ−s(x)|]≤a(n). The source’s communication cost is a worst-case bound on the number of bits a user sends, including all its messages; for a fixed m-message encoder over a k-symbol alphabet the natural bit representation costs m⌈log₂k⌉. Expected message counts are a different resource. In the contemplated extension, later messages may depend on earlier communication, but the cited question does not define the permitted dependencies or what information survives each shuffle. Constants in the displayed lower bound may depend on fixed privacy and error-exponent parameters but not on n.',question_type='asymptotic_complexity',answer_criterion='After the missing protocol class and quantitative target are fixed, establish the required communication lower bound for every protocol in that class, with privacy and accuracy exactly as stated, or refute that particular bound by a protocol satisfying those conditions. A noninteractive lower bound alone, a local-model lower bound, or an expected-communication guarantee does not establish a worst-case lower bound with interaction. The present source question has no fully specified acceptance threshold.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Anonymity changes the communication–accuracy tradeoff',text='The 2020 paper gives pure-private binary summation with expected absolute error Oε(1) and Oε(log n) one-bit messages per user. Its lower bound requires Ωε(√log n) worst-case bits per user even when substantially larger sub-square-root error is allowed. Both statements concern one round of user messages.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The missing antecedent was communication',text='The extracted sentence originally omitted the resource, task and privacy guarantee. It follows a question about whether interaction can beat the noninteractive communication bound. The alternative request asks for some lower bound with interaction, without committing to the same asymptotic strength.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Later optimal-error protocols use expected communication',text='Ghazi, Kumar and Manurangsi obtain noninteractive binary summation with root-mean-square error O(1/ε) and expected O((log n)/ε) one-bit messages per user for bounded positive ε. The worst-case number of messages is unbounded. Their 2024 discussion explicitly distinguishes lower bounds for expected communication from the earlier worst-case bound; this does not settle the interactive question.',citation='aggregation2024')],why='Would clarify whether adaptive anonymous communication can avoid the communication overhead of accurate pure-private aggregation, and which limitations come from privacy versus the one-round restriction.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2020',text='Noninteractive constant-error protocols and a worst-case communication lower bound are proved; an interactive lower bound is left as an open direction.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2024',text='Optimal-order error is attained with logarithmic expected communication for fixed ε, still without interaction and with unbounded worst-case message count.',citation='aggregation2024')],status='source_open',status_note='Read the original conference and full-version communication statements and concluding question, plus the 2024 paper’s main theorem and open questions. Later-work searches through 12 September 2026 found no result settling a specified general interactive version. The main obstacle to certification here is the source’s missing interactive model and lower-bound threshold.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,statement_review=dict(reviewed_on=DATE,status='needs_specification',notes=['Recovered binary summation, pure transcript privacy, expected absolute error and worst-case per-user bits.'],remaining_issue=issue),importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='Tests whether interaction changes the privacy–communication separation for the basic aggregation primitive. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note=issue,working_summary=dict(sentences=['Each user holds one bit and an analyst must estimate the sum from anonymous messages.','Pure differential privacy protects the entire transcript when one user’s bit changes.','The original lower bound concerns worst-case bits per user in noninteractive protocols with sub-square-root expected absolute error.','The question asks for a communication lower bound that also permits interaction.','The interactive model and the strength of a nontrivial bound must still be fixed; later bounds on expected communication do not resolve those choices.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','full','aggregation2024'])),notes=['Restored communication resource, binary input and exact privacy/error antecedent.','Separated worst-case bit cost from expected message counts and final-output privacy from transcript privacy.','Recorded the unspecified interaction class and lower-bound target without inventing a theorem.','Checked full version and 2024 optimal-error aggregation result.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs],state='improved_unfinished')

i='TCS-3392';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 1, online/recourse definitions and Theorems 1–2; Section 5, second open question.'
refs += [dict(id='journal2025',title='Online Metric Matching on the Line with Recourse',authors='Nicole Megow; Lukas Nölke',year=2025,url='https://doi.org/10.1007/s00453-025-01299-8',locator='Section 1.1, Theorems 1–3; Section 5, constant recourse per request.'),dict(id='stability2026',title='On the Stability of Minimum-Weight Perfect Matching on the Line',authors='Mark de Berg; Ulrike Schmidt-Kraepelin; Andree-Ovidiu Ștef',year=2026,url='https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.ESA.2026.27',locator='Section 1, especially page 27:3: per-update stability and paired server/client insertions in the bipartite variant.')]
save(i,dict(title='Constant lifetime recourse for competitive online matching on the line',formal='Does there exist a deterministic online algorithm A and absolute constants α≥1 and B∈N such that A maintains an α-competitive minimum-cost matching on the line while reassigning every individual request at most B times over its entire lifetime? For every finite known set S of labeled servers with real locations, and every sequence r₁,…,rₙ of requests with real locations, n≤|S|, A must after each arrival t output an injection M_t:{r₁,…,r_t}→S. Require Σ_{i≤t}|rᵢ−M_t(rᵢ)|≤α OPT_t at every t, and for each i require |{t:i<t≤n and M_t(rᵢ)≠M_{t−1}(rᵢ)}|≤B. Here OPT_t is the minimum cost of an injection of the first t requests into S. The constants and the algorithm are independent of n, the locations, their spread and the arrival order.',definitions='A server or request has an identity and a location on the real line; expressions inside absolute values denote locations. Every request can use every server, with edge cost their distance. As in the source’s positive-cost model, request–server distances are positive; no lower bound on them or on the ratio of largest to smallest distance is promised. All server locations are revealed before the first request. A new request reveals only itself; future requests are unknown, and the algorithm must finish its update before the next one arrives. At most one request occupies a server. Previously matched requests may be assigned to different servers during an update, with simultaneous replacement of the matching permitted. The first assignment of a new request is not a reassignment. The displayed lifetime count concerns changes between maintained matchings and excludes internal auxiliary computations. It bounds each request separately, not just the sum divided by n, and it does not impose a constant limit on how many different requests can move at a single arrival. Competitive cost is the total length of the current matching, with no additive term and no sum over previous matching costs. Determinism follows the model of the source’s exhibited algorithms and is made explicit as an editorial convention; randomized expected guarantees constitute a separate extension. The target restricts recourse and competitive ratio, not update running time; a uniform algorithm may perform finite exact arithmetic and comparisons on revealed real coordinates.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Give one online algorithm with universal constants α,B satisfying both inequalities for every instance and every prefix, or prove that no such deterministic algorithm and constants exist. A constant bound only on average reassignments, a bound per arrival without control of each request’s lifetime, or a guarantee only for alternating instances is not the stated result. An impossibility result must cover all deterministic algorithms in this model, not just the source’s algorithm.',context_blocks=[dict(title='A stronger requirement than amortized recourse',text='The source obtains a constant competitive ratio with O(log n) average reassignments per request. This controls the total number of changes; it need not keep any particular request from moving many times. Its second concluding question asks whether a constant number per request can suffice, as in its alternating-instance result. The first concluding question, about recourse available per iteration, is distinct.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The alternating case gives the lifetime interpretation',text='An instance is alternating when there is a server between any two request locations. The 2025 journal version gives a (1+ε)-competitive algorithm in this special case that reassigns each request O(1/ε²) times, and explicitly leaves constant recourse for general instances open. This per-request lifetime guarantee supplies the interpretation used in the statement.',citation='journal2025'),dict(title='The journal tradeoff does not give both constants',text='For 1≤f(n)≤log n, the journal version gives competitive ratio O(f(n)) with amortized recourse O(log n/f(n)). Taking f constant retains logarithmic amortized recourse; taking f logarithmic retains a logarithmic competitive ratio. The theorem therefore does not provide simultaneous constant competitiveness and constant lifetime recourse.',citation='journal2025'),dict(title='A 2026 stability lower bound uses a different arrival model',text='De Berg, Schmidt-Kraepelin and Ștef prove that sublogarithmic per-update stability cannot maintain a bounded approximation in their matching model. Even its bipartite version inserts a server and a client together. It is not the present model with all servers known initially, and its per-update cost is different from a lifetime bound for each request.',citation='stability2026')],why='Would determine whether online uncertainty in a basic geometric assignment problem can be absorbed by only a bounded number of revisions to each individual decision.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2020',text='The source gives constant competitiveness with logarithmic amortized recourse and constant lifetime recourse for alternating instances.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2025-03-01',text='The journal version retains the general constant-recourse question and adds a competitive-ratio/amortized-recourse tradeoff.',citation='journal2025'),dict(date='2026-08-25',text='A related per-update stability lower bound allows paired arrivals of servers and clients; it does not settle the fixed-server lifetime-recourse target.',citation='stability2026')],status='source_open',status_note='Checked the original and 2025 journal problem statements, model, alternating-instance theorem and concluding question. Checked the exact arrival/stability model of ESA 2026.27. A 2026 malicious matching preprint surfaced in searches but concerns maximum cardinality and externally replaced matchings, not this cost problem. No matching resolution found through 12 September 2026; searches are not exhaustive.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='Asks whether constant individual decision instability suffices for competitive geometric assignment. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Recovered the full source question and interpreted per-request recourse using the alternating-instance theorem. Made deterministic guarantees explicit; distinguished lifetime, amortized and per-arrival budgets, and checked the 2025 journal and 2026 stability result.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['All servers on the real line are known before requests arrive.','Each new request must be matched immediately, while earlier assignments can be revised.','The question asks for a constant competitive ratio and a constant bound on the lifetime number of moves of every request.','The known general result bounds average recourse by O(log n), whereas alternating instances admit constant recourse for each request.','The 2025 journal version still asks for the general improvement, and the 2026 stability lower bound uses a different arrival model and budget.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','journal2025','stability2026'])),notes=['Restored the full general line-matching question and fixed-server online model.','Defined prefix competitiveness and constant lifetime recourse for each request, distinct from both other budget conventions.','Recorded the deterministic editorial convention without claiming a randomized equivalence.','Updated journal progress and distinguished the 2026 paired-arrival stability lower bound.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://jbaligacs.github.io/'])
